m:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139534387 17592186045417 false] [17592186314869 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}},\\,y(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186332840 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" 13194139534387 17592186045417 false] [17592186332840 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186357737 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=32t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+16t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+8t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534387 17592186045417 false] [17592186357737 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=32t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+16t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+8t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.