417 true] [17592186420493 1079 #uuid "dc977685-7a6f-5892-9d86-65a9ccbd87da" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420481 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420482 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420485 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420488 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1077 "field-3" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420481 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420494 1079 #uuid "e0f59fc3-d642-5f6b-9b06-fc7d67132d2a" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420490 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420491 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420492 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420494 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1069 "q-343408" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1071 "Consider the function $F(x)={x}^{2}$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\nThe Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1072 #uuid "17f63cde-bca7-5760-8481-ec7b747c5fc4" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420495 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & =-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420493 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420496 1069 "q-226151" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420496 1071 "![](images/de/de3e018fa1382ee2250d1b2e256e3b47cafef346852007f0e6afb84342d7cb04.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420496 1072 #uuid "6071adb1-29d3-5cda-a599-699b41d64b95" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true] [17592186420496 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/0950c559d5d6df93cf40afd8fdbd07f090f864fa462083bfd496246e7f2bded9.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-24.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-24)=24.\n$$" 13194139534387 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-09eccf47-a4e8-5569-a8eb-3f4ce0ab918c" 17592186420471 "answer-0aa1db5b-3f41-577b-8ee0-5f7105de71c4" 17592186420472 "answer-0c5de29c-bfc1-53ce-83a7-41993dcf260c" 17592186420473 "answer-1422be90-a47c-526f-8a9e-b95f92cd8fb6" 17592186420474 "answer-1616afa8-cded-5301-a526-ce193f44941a" 17592186420475 "answer-2cec1b04-11f1-52b4-bede-6fa1be707451" 17592186420476 "answer-2da9dc18-ca7c-5322-8bbc-e34d0950137f" 17592186420477 "answer-3233da4d-abf4-509b-8a83-199acf697664" 17592186420478 "answer-56ce5d4f-b6f2-580a-8157-fe69a694922d" 17592186420479 "answer-615383ae-5073-5a27-a6bc-b222285b07e6" 17592186420480 "answer-6bdb7dff-5315-5b34-963b-b23231924305" 17592186420481 "answer-7854e4e4-3afd-59af-9211-a9f390e0d5e3" 17592186420482 "answer-7860d733-39e7-5d34-930a-375944911a4e" 17592186420483 "answer-87300a85-1a3b-5482-8d6a-77042fc3955d" 17592186420484 "answer-87351955-ce38-552a-af85-01e48cfc1d09" 17592186420485 "answer-a9e0b03b-9a26-5f35-8452-4cc76772c98a" 17592186420486 "answer-c8c2a375-3ff9-5e2d-be12-112ac4c6827d" 17592186420487 "answer-cd1bf197-8595-5dd9-872f-af28d256f5d9" 17592186420488 "answer-f988be9c-3e67-5b8b-a16b-351d7db22e25" 17592186420489 "answer-field-4a2d1532-e62f-56cf-ad90-cfb160e1a05b" 17592186420490 "answer-field-50e9e76b-dcdf-5c6c-a4fa-6c010f2f834f" 17592186420491 "answer-field-db2e5bb2-a3cb-5379-a3ce-0ec72000811e" 17592186420492 "answer-field-dc977685-7a6f-5892-9d86-65a9ccbd87da" 17592186420493 "answer-field-e0f59fc3-d642-5f6b-9b06-fc7d67132d2a" 17592186420494 "question-17f63cde-bca7-5760-8481-ec7b747c5fc4" 17592186420495 "question-6071adb1-29